        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Socialism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1827 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Thinkers</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-de-saint-simon">Henri de Saint-Simon</a>, who proposed economic planning and scientific administration; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-owen">Robert Owen</a>, one of the fathers of the cooperative movement; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-fourier">Charles Fourier</a>; Pierre Leroux, who claimed the first use of the word; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Friedrich Engels</a>, who called their doctrine scientific socialism; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-lassalle">Ferdinand Lassalle</a>, first to set out state socialism; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eduard-bernstein">Eduard Bernstein</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-stuart-mill">John Stuart Mill</a>, of ethical and liberal socialism</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Key texts</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-communist-manifesto">The Communist Manifesto</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Friedrich Engels</a> (1847–48); <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-stuart-mill">John Stuart Mill</a>’s Principles of Political Economy (1848); August Bebel’s Woman under Socialism (1879); Friedrich Engels’s The Origin of the Family, Private Property, and the State (1884); Oscar Wilde’s The Soul of Man Under Socialism; Edward Carpenter’s The Intermediate Sex (1908)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Core ideas</span><span class="info-stat-val">social ownership of the means of production, whether public, cooperative, community or employee; production for use rather than for profit; economic planning, or markets among socially owned enterprises; social equality and social justice; workers’ self-management and economic democracy; reform or revolution as the road to a post-capitalist society</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Today</span><span class="info-stat-val">socialist parties head governments in several countries; social democratic and socialist parties are networked in the Socialist International, the Party of European Socialists (1992) and the Progressive Alliance (2013); the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a> Forum (1990) links <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>’s left-wing socialist parties; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>’s economy is formally a socialist market economy; a revival in the 2010s around Podemos, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriza">Syriza</a>, Die Linke, La <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> Insoumise, Jeremy Corbyn and Bernie Sanders</span></div>
