    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The word first appeared in the 1820s and 1830s: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-owen">Robert Owen</a>’s Co-Operative Magazine spoke of socialists in 1827, and Pierre Leroux claimed to have introduced socialism in the Parisian journal Le Globe in 1832.</li><li>By the early 1920s the international socialist movement had divided into its two dominant tendencies, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-democracy">social democracy</a>, and socialism had become the most influential secular movement of the 20th century.</li><li>Its forms differ over markets and planning and over who owns and runs enterprises, from Soviet central planning and Yugoslav self-management to the Nordic mixed economies and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>’s socialist market economy.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>For one week in December 1899 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australian-labor-party">Australian Labor Party</a> formed the first socialist government in the world, in the Colony of Queensland, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anderson-dawson">Anderson Dawson</a> as premier.</p></aside>
    </section>

